\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
%
% TRANSFORMATION TDoBeDeletion
< m1: I4::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
                  [ I1:mu1,
                    auxrel/VERB{VERBrec1}
                          [head/SUBVERB{SUBVERBrec1}
                               [head/BVERB(KEY_doBVERBkey){BVERBrec1}]
                          ],
                    I2:mu2,
                    predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                           [ I3:mu3,
                             head/I5::VERB{VERBrec2}[mu6],
                             I7:mu4
                           ],
                    mu5
                  ]
>

< m : I4::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
                  [ I1:mu1,
                    auxrel/I6::VERB{VERBrec3}[mu6],
                    I2:mu2,
                    predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                           [ I3:mu3,
                             I7:mu4
                           ],
                    mu5
                  ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: ALL (mu1, I11:rel1/..) AND 
           NOT EXIST (mu1, [auxrel/..])
         I11: QUOTE_SRelPrec(rel1, auxrel)
       I2: ALL (mu2, I21:rel2/..)
         I21: rel2 IN AUX_postAuxAdvRels OR
              rel2 = negrel
       I3: ALL (mu3, I31:rel3/..)
       I4: SENTENCErec1.finiteness = finite
       I5: VERBrec2.subc = beverb
       I6: VERBrec3.subc IN [beverb, haveverb]
       I7: NOT EXIST (mu4, [I31:rel3/..])
         I31: rel3 IN AUX_BeginVPAdvRels
       m1: SENTENCErec1.modus <> imperative 
       m : SENTENCErec1.modus <> imperative 
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec1 = COPYT_subverbTOverb(SUBVERBrec1) AND
              SUBVERBrec1 = COPYT_bverbTOsubverb(BVERBrec1) AND
              VERBrec2 = { persons : []
                           numbers : []
                           modus   : infinitive
                           tense   : omegatense }
          A1: VERBrec3 := VERBrec2;
              VERBrec3 := { persons : VERBrec1.persons
                            numbers : VERBrec1.numbers
                            modus   : VERBrec1.modus
                            tense   : VERBrec1.tense }
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: SUBVERBrec1 := COPYT_bverbTOsubverb(BVERBrec1);
              VERBrec1 := COPYT_subverbTOverb(SUBVERBrec1);
              VERBrec1 := { persons : VERBrec3.persons
                            numbers : VERBrec3.numbers
                            modus   : VERBrec3.modus
                            tense   : VERBrec3.tense };
              VERBrec2 := VERBrec3;
              VERBrec2 := { persons : []
                            numbers : []
                            modus   : infinitive
                            tense   : omegatense };
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TDoBeDeletion
\item[* relevant examples:] He does be a doctor $\rightarrow$ He is a doctor;
(in analysis only): He does have a book $\rightarrow$ He has a book
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_DoBeDeletion
\item[* date of creation:] 11-AUG-1988 15:00:20  
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 11/12/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted to stop imperatives with a negation; they must keep {\em be: do not be 
silly, $^{*}$be not silly\/}
\item date: 07/06/91\ \ \ \ \ author: Petra de Wit\\
Adapted to stop all imperatives, since {\em be} should not be raised into 
auxrel but remain inside VP in positive imperatives as well as negative.
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to delete the auxiliary `do' for verbs that don't need it, viz.\ 
{\em be\/} and (in analysis only) {\em have\/}. Deletion must take place prior 
to TC\_AuxToComp, or the auxiliary `do' will end up in the Comp-position, 
instead of the main verb. 
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] In generation, {\em have
\/} is treated as an ordinary main verb, always taking the auxiliary `do'. In 
analysis, it is possible for `have' to show inversion without the use of `do':
{\em Has he a book?\/} (Quirk, esp.\ BrE, in the stative sense of possession, 
\S 3.18 and \S 7.59). Since this sentence will still be dealt with by 
TNoDeletion (any verb preceding the subject is considered in conjrel by the 
surface parser), it will not receive `do' there, and will end up without any 
head after TAuxToComp has put `have' in auxrel. This is considered undesirable.
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}
